  \subsection{Design Improvements for the Mars Rubber-Glass House –
  Proposals for
  v2}\label{design-improvements-for-the-mars-rubber-glass-house-proposals-for-v2}
  
  \subsubsection{Introduction}\label{introduction-3}
  
  The original design of the 1000 m² Mars Rubber-Glass House
  (\href{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16493055}{DOI:
  10.5281/zenodo.16493055}) establishes a solid foundation for Martian
  habitat construction using silicone rubber, borosilicate glass, and
  titanium/Inconel framing. However, the v1 design focuses primarily on
  structural feasibility and cost, while several aspects critical to
  long-term habitability remain unaddressed. This document proposes
  specific improvements for a v2 revision, informed by ISS operational
  experience, NASA’s Baseline Values and Assumptions Document (BVAD),
  Biosphere 2 lessons learned, and current Mars mission architectures.
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
  \subsubsection{1. Interior Height: 3 m → 5
  m}\label{interior-height-3-m-5-m}
  
  \paragraph{Problem}\label{problem}
  
  The v1 design specifies a 3 m ceiling height — adequate for basic
  operations but psychologically constraining for permanent habitation.
  Research on confined environments (submarines, Antarctic stations, ISS)
  consistently shows that perceived spaciousness is a key factor in crew
  morale during long-duration missions.
  
  \paragraph{Proposal}\label{proposal}
  
  Increase the single-storey interior height to \textbf{5 m}, enabling:
  
  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{Mezzanine levels} for private sleeping quarters (2.20 m
    headroom sufficient)
  \item
    \textbf{Vertical farming racks} on 3 tiers within the agricultural
    zone
  \item
    \textbf{Improved air circulation} — larger volume reduces CO\(_2\)
    hotspots and thermal stratification
  \item
    \textbf{Psychological benefit} — sense of openness critical for
    multi-year stays
  \end{itemize}
  
  \paragraph{Material Impact}\label{material-impact}
  
  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}llll@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  Parameter & v1 (3 m) & v2 (5 m) & Delta \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  Interior volume & 3,000 m³ & 5,000 m³ & +67\% \\
  Glass wall area & \textasciitilde420 m² & \textasciitilde700 m² &
  +67\% \\
  Frame weight & 5,500 kg & \textasciitilde8,500 kg & +55\% \\
  Atmosphere mass (at 70 kPa) & \textasciitilde2.6 t & \textasciitilde4.3
  t & +67\% \\
  Foundation wall height & 0.5 m & 0.5 m & unchanged \\
  \end{longtable}
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
  \subsubsection{2. Thermal Zoning: 1 Zone → 3
  Zones}\label{thermal-zoning-1-zone-3-zones}
  
  \paragraph{Problem}\label{problem-1}
  
  The v1 design treats the entire 1000 m² as a single climate volume.
  Heating 5,000 m³ uniformly to 22 °C is energy-intensive and suboptimal —
  plants benefit from cooler temperatures, and laboratories may need
  stable conditions different from living areas.
  
  \paragraph{Proposal}\label{proposal-1}
  
  Introduce \textbf{three climate zones} separated by lightweight silicone
  foam partition walls:
  
  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}
    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}
    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}
    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}
    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
  Zone
  \end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
  Area
  \end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
  Temperature
  \end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
  Purpose
  \end{minipage} \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  \textbf{Warm zone} & \textasciitilde350 m² & 22–24 °C & Living quarters,
  sleeping cabins, kitchen \\
  \textbf{Temperate zone} & \textasciitilde250 m² & 18–20 °C &
  Laboratories, workshops, machinery \\
  \textbf{Cool zone} & \textasciitilde400 m² & 12–16 °C & Agriculture
  (cool-night cycles for crops) \\
  \end{longtable}
  
  \textbf{Energy savings:} Estimated 20–30\% reduction in heating load
  compared to uniform heating, as the agricultural zone (largest area)
  operates at lower temperatures.
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
  \subsubsection{3. Water Management
  System}\label{water-management-system}
  
  \paragraph{Problem}\label{problem-2}
  
  The v1 design does not address water supply, recycling, or humidity
  control. Water is the single most critical resource for crew survival
  and plant growth.
  
  \paragraph{Proposal}\label{proposal-2}
  
  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{Closed-loop water recycling:} Condensate recovery from air,
    greywater filtration, and urine processing (similar to ISS WRS — Water
    Recovery System)
  \item
    \textbf{Target humidity:} 40–60\% relative humidity for optimal plant
    growth and human comfort
  \item
    \textbf{Underfloor reservoir:} 10 m³ water tank integrated into the
    silicone rubber floor slab — the material is inherently watertight
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Provides \textasciitilde6 months emergency reserve for 4 crew
      members
    \item
      Thermal mass helps stabilize interior temperatures
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Estimated water budget:} \textasciitilde25 L/person/day (with
    recycling) vs.~\textasciitilde50 L/day without recycling
  \end{itemize}
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
  \subsubsection{4. Radiation Shielding}\label{radiation-shielding}
  
  \paragraph{Problem}\label{problem-3}
  
  The v1 design mentions UV-protective glass coatings but does not address
  galactic cosmic radiation (GCR) or solar particle events (SPE). Mars
  lacks a global magnetic field, resulting in surface doses of
  \textasciitilde0.67 mSv/day (MSL/RAD measurement). Over a 2-year stay,
  this accumulates to \textasciitilde490 mSv — close to NASA’s career
  limits.
  
  \paragraph{Proposal}\label{proposal-3}
  
  A layered shielding approach:
  
  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{Regolith layer on the roof} (above solar panels):
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      50 cm of Martian regolith ≈ 80 g/cm² mass shielding
    \item
      Reduces GCR dose by \textasciitilde50\%
    \item
      Requires regolith harvesting robotics (cross-reference:
      \href{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15455156}{DOI:
      10.5281/zenodo.15455156} – Mars Field Laboratories)
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Water wall panels} in side walls:
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      10–20 cm water layer between double-glass panels
    \item
      Excellent hydrogen-rich shielding against secondary neutrons
    \item
      Maintains transparency while providing thermal mass
    \item
      Estimated shielding: additional 20–30\% GCR dose reduction
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Storm shelter} for SPE events:
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      One reinforced room (\textasciitilde20 m²) with ≥2 m regolith
      overhead
    \item
      Designed for 4 people for up to 72 hours
    \item
      Includes emergency life support, water, and communications
    \end{itemize}
  \end{enumerate}
  
  \paragraph{Combined shielding
  estimate}\label{combined-shielding-estimate}
  
  Effective dose reduction to \textasciitilde0.20–0.25 mSv/day (from
  0.67), well within acceptable limits for multi-year habitation.
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
  \subsubsection{5. Agricultural System
  Expansion}\label{agricultural-system-expansion}
  
  \paragraph{Problem}\label{problem-4}
  
  The v1 design allocates 50 m² per person (200 m² total) for food
  production. Research from NASA BVAD and Biosphere 2 suggests 80–120 m²
  per person is more realistic for full caloric autonomy under
  controlled-environment agriculture.
  
  \paragraph{Proposal}\label{proposal-4}
  
  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{Increase base agricultural area} to 400 m² (100 m²/person)
  \item
    \textbf{Vertical farming on 3 tiers} within the 5 m height → effective
    growing area of \textasciitilde1,200 m²
  \item
    \textbf{LED supplemental lighting:} Mars receives only
    \textasciitilde43\% of Earth’s solar irradiance; supplemental
    full-spectrum LEDs are essential for crop yields, especially in winter
    or during dust storms
  \item
    \textbf{Algae bioreactors:} Expand from 20 m² to 40 m² — algae produce
    more O\(_2\) and protein per m² than any crop
  \item
    \textbf{Aquaponics integration:} Closed-loop fish–plant system for
    protein diversification and nutrient cycling
  \item
    \textbf{Crop selection:} Potatoes, soybeans, wheat, lettuce, tomatoes,
    spirulina (optimized for caloric density and growth speed)
  \end{itemize}
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
  \subsubsection{6. Airlock Redundancy: 1 → 2 + Emergency
  Hatch}\label{airlock-redundancy-1-2-emergency-hatch}
  
  \paragraph{Problem}\label{problem-5}
  
  The v1 design has a single airlock — a critical single point of failure.
  If the airlock jams, leaks, or is blocked by debris, the crew has no EVA
  capability and no emergency exit.
  
  \paragraph{Proposal}\label{proposal-5}
  
  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{2 full-size airlocks} at opposite ends of the structure (north
    and south)
  \item
    \textbf{1 emergency hatch} (1 m × 1 m) for evacuation only
  \item
    Each airlock: 2 m × 2 m × 5 m (unchanged from v1 spec)
  \item
    Total added weight: \textasciitilde1,000 kg (one additional airlock)
  \end{itemize}
  
  Note: The v1 variants document already specifies 2–3 airlocks for
  multi-storey configurations. This should be the baseline for all
  designs.
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
  \subsubsection{7. Energy Storage and
  Redundancy}\label{energy-storage-and-redundancy}
  
  \paragraph{Problem}\label{problem-6}
  
  The v1 design relies exclusively on rooftop solar panels (973,392
  kWh/Mars year). Mars dust storms can last weeks, reducing solar output
  to 10–20\% of nominal. No energy storage is specified.
  
  \paragraph{Proposal}\label{proposal-6}
  
  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{Battery bank:} Lithium-iron-phosphate (LFP) batteries for at
    least \textbf{72 hours} of full autonomy without solar input
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Estimated capacity: \textasciitilde80–100 kWh usable storage
    \item
      Weight: \textasciitilde800 kg
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Radioisotope heater units (RHU):} Backup heat source for
    critical systems during prolonged dust storms (similar to Mars 2020
    MMRTG concept)
  \item
    \textbf{Load prioritization:} During storms, non-essential systems
    (some lighting, laboratory equipment) are powered down; life support
    and agriculture lighting take priority
  \end{itemize}
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
  \subsubsection{8. Acoustics and Privacy}\label{acoustics-and-privacy}
  
  \paragraph{Problem}\label{problem-7}
  
  The v1 design describes an open floor plan for 4 people. ISS crew
  reports and Antarctic winter-over studies consistently identify
  \textbf{noise and lack of privacy} as top stressors in confined
  environments.
  
  \paragraph{Proposal}\label{proposal-7}
  
  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{Individual crew cabins:} 4 modules, each \textasciitilde12 m²
    (3 m × 4 m), with:
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Silicone foam sound insulation (STC rating ≥ 35)
    \item
      Personal lighting and climate control
    \item
      Door or curtain for visual privacy
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Quiet room:} One additional 12 m² enclosed space for
    relaxation, reading, video calls
  \item
    \textbf{Acoustic dampening:} Silicone foam panels on ceilings and
    partition walls to reduce reverberation (the glass+rubber structure is
    inherently reflective)
  \end{itemize}
  
