  To provide reasonable cost estimations for both the \textbf{Test Project
  on Earth} and the \textbf{First Building Project on Mars}, we’ll make
  assumptions about costs related to materials, assembly, transport (in
  the case of Mars), and certification. These estimates will focus on
  medium-range costs with the necessary contingencies for unforeseen
  challenges. All costs are in USD and assume current technology and
  prices.
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
  \subsection{\texorpdfstring{1. \textbf{Test Project on
  Earth}}{1. Test Project on Earth}}\label{test-project-on-earth}
  
  This project will involve building a full-scale prototype on Earth to
  test materials and certify the structural integrity and engineering of
  the rubber-glass house. This project would exclude interiors but would
  serve as the basis for validating the Mars design.
  
  \subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{Estimated Costs for Earth-based
  Test
  Project:}}{Estimated Costs for Earth-based Test Project:}}\label{estimated-costs-for-earth-based-test-project}
  
  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{Silicone rubber and additives:}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Cost: \textasciitilde\$50,000\\
    \item
      Comments: Materials sourced locally, includes prototyping and slight
      overages for testing.
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Titanium/Inconel frame:}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Cost: \textasciitilde\$175,000\\
    \item
      Comments: Titanium and Inconel are expensive, but Earth-based
      manufacturing benefits from established supply chains and
      technologies.
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Borosilicate glass (with insulation):}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Cost: \textasciitilde\$450,000\\
    \item
      Comments: Pricing is slightly lower than Mars due to ease of local
      fabrication.
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Solar panels for the roof:}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Cost: \textasciitilde\$200,000\\
    \item
      Comments: Standard high-efficiency solar panels, including some
      custom work for design integration.
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Airlock (Titanium/Inconel \& Borosilicate):}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Cost: \textasciitilde\$120,000\\
    \item
      Comments: Prototyping a functional airlock to simulate Martian
      conditions.
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Manufacturing and assembly on Earth:}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Cost: \textasciitilde\$300,000\\
    \item
      Comments: Includes labor, equipment, and custom engineering
      solutions. Could be cheaper if using automated or prefabricated
      methods.
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Testing, certification, and validation (Earth environment):}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Cost: \textasciitilde\$400,000\\
    \item
      Comments: Extensive testing, including structural integrity, thermal
      resistance, airtightness, and durability.
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Interior (medium-range budget for labs, living spaces, etc.):}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Cost: \textasciitilde\$200,000\\
    \item
      Comments: Basic interior build-out, equivalent to a medium-range,
      concrete-based setup.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{itemize}
  
  \subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{Total Estimated Cost for Earth
  Test
  Project:}}{Total Estimated Cost for Earth Test Project:}}\label{total-estimated-cost-for-earth-test-project}
  
  \textbf{\textasciitilde\$1,895,000}
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
  \subsection{\texorpdfstring{2. \textbf{First Building Project on Mars
  (Including
  Transport)}}{2. First Building Project on Mars (Including Transport)}}\label{first-building-project-on-mars-including-transport}
  
  This includes building the structure on Mars with all necessary
  transportation and construction considerations. All costs assume current
  transportation costs to Mars and exclude the interiors, which would be
  addressed separately.
  
  \subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{Estimated Costs for Mars Project
  (Excluding
  Interiors):}}{Estimated Costs for Mars Project (Excluding Interiors):}}\label{estimated-costs-for-mars-project-excluding-interiors}
  
  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{Silicone rubber and additives (local production potential):}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Cost: \textasciitilde\$75,000\\
    \item
      Comments: If silicone rubber can be partially manufactured from
      Martian resources, this cost could decrease. Otherwise, transport of
      this material would increase the cost significantly.
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Titanium/Inconel frame (including transport):}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Material cost: \textasciitilde\$175,000\\
    \item
      Transport cost: \textasciitilde\$10,000,000\\
    \item
      Comments: Given the high weight of these materials, transportation
      would dominate the cost. The price assumes SpaceX or a comparable
      provider offering lower-cost Mars missions (\$2,700/kg).
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Borosilicate glass (including transport):}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Material cost: \textasciitilde\$500,000\\
    \item
      Transport cost: \textasciitilde\$27,000,000\\
    \item
      Comments: Borosilicate glass is heavy, and transporting 10,000 kg at
      \textasciitilde\$2,700/kg results in substantial costs.
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Solar panels (including transport):}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Material cost: \textasciitilde\$200,000\\
    \item
      Transport cost: \textasciitilde\$2,000,000\\
    \item
      Comments: Solar panels are lighter than glass but still expensive to
      transport in large quantities. There may be opportunities to produce
      some materials locally on Mars in the future.
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Airlock (including transport):}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Material cost: \textasciitilde\$120,000\\
    \item
      Transport cost: \textasciitilde\$2,700,000\\
    \item
      Comments: The airlock is a crucial element that must be transported
      in a durable form. The cost reflects its weight and specialized
      nature.
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Manufacturing and assembly on Mars (robotics and human
    assistance):}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Cost: \textasciitilde\$2,000,000\\
    \item
      Comments: Includes costs for deploying robotic construction teams,
      supervision, and other logistical challenges unique to Mars.
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Interior (excluded from transport):}
  
    \begin{itemize}
    \tightlist
    \item
      Cost: \textasciitilde\$300,000\\
    \item
      Comments: This is an estimate for fitting out interiors with
      medium-range construction, similar to what you might expect from
      typical Earth-based housing, adjusted for Martian conditions.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{itemize}
  
  \subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{\textbf{Total Estimated Cost for First
  Mars Project (Excluding
  Interiors):}}{Total Estimated Cost for First Mars Project (Excluding Interiors):}}\label{total-estimated-cost-for-first-mars-project-excluding-interiors}
  
  \textbf{\textasciitilde\$44,870,000}
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
