  \section{Introduction}\label{introduction}
  
  \subsection{Why Mars?}\label{why-mars}
  
  Human settlement of Mars is no longer a question of \emph{if} but of
  \emph{when}. With launch costs falling rapidly — SpaceX’s Starship
  targets below \$200/kg to low Earth orbit — the economic barrier to
  interplanetary construction is eroding faster than most roadmaps
  predicted.
  
  Yet the engineering challenges remain formidable. Mars offers a surface
  gravity of 0.38 g, an atmosphere of 95 \% CO\_\{2\} at less than 1 \% of
  Earth’s sea-level pressure, temperatures swinging between −60 °C and +20
  °C, and a radiation environment unshielded by any significant
  magnetosphere.
  
  \subsection{Scope of This Book}\label{scope-of-this-book}
  
  This volume collects the author’s work across three complementary
  pillars of Mars surface operations:
  
  \subsubsection{Part I — Mars Habitats}\label{part-i-mars-habitats}
  
  The centrepiece is the \emph{Rubber-Glass House} concept: a large-scale
  inflatable habitat whose transparent ETFE membrane admits enough
  photosynthetically active radiation (PAR) to sustain indoor agriculture
  while a layered silica-aerogel insulation system maintains habitable
  temperatures with minimal energy input. Five chapters trace the concept
  from first design through variants, cost estimation and a
  community-oriented “People’s House” proposal to concrete v2 design
  improvements informed by habitability engineering.
  
  \subsubsection{Part II — Biomimetic and Self-Growing
  Structures}\label{part-ii-biomimetic-and-self-growing-structures}
  
  A single visionary paper sketches a three-step evolution: from classical
  inflatable construction, through biomimetic silica-aerogel composites
  inspired by diatoms and radiolaria, to a speculative stage in which
  habitats grow autonomously from regolith feedstock using programmed
  silicon-based polymers.
  
  \subsubsection{Part III — Mars Robotics}\label{part-iii-mars-robotics}
  
  Before humans set foot on the surface, autonomous systems must survey
  terrain, deploy infrastructure and maintain equipment. The \emph{Mars
  Field Laboratories} chapter proposes teams of legged robots and
  cooperative swarm clusters that operate as mobile science platforms.
  
  \subsection{How to Read This Book}\label{how-to-read-this-book}
  
  Each chapter is self-contained and was written — or can be read — as an
  independent paper. Cross-references link related concepts across
  chapters. Published papers carry their Zenodo DOI in the chapter header
  for citation purposes.
  
  The appendices include the full license text and a colophon documenting
  the toolchain used to produce this book.
  
