  \paragraph{6.3 Storm Shelter}\label{storm-shelter}
  
  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{Location:} Central portion of temperate zone
  \item
    \textbf{Area:} \textasciitilde20 m² (5 m × 4 m)
  \item
    \textbf{Capacity:} 4 crew for up to 72 hours
  \item
    \textbf{Shielding:} ≥ 2 m regolith overhead + water walls on all sides
  \item
    \textbf{Equipment:} Emergency life support, 200 L water, food rations
    (72 h), medical kit, communications terminal
  \item
    \textbf{Effective dose rate in shelter:} \textless{} 0.05 mSv/day
  \end{itemize}
  
  \paragraph{6.4 Combined Shielding
  Performance}\label{combined-shielding-performance}
  
  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}lll@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  Location & Dose Rate (mSv/day) & Annual Dose (mSv) \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  Unshielded Mars surface & 0.67 & 244 \\
  Inside habitat (general) & \textasciitilde0.20–0.25 &
  \textasciitilde73–91 \\
  Inside storm shelter & \textless{} 0.05 & \textless{} 18 \\
  NASA career limit reference & — & 600 \\
  \end{longtable}
  
  A 2-year stay at \textasciitilde0.22 mSv/day = \textasciitilde160 mSv —
  well within NASA career limits.
  
