  \subsubsection{7. Crew Routines — Operational
  Schedules}\label{crew-routines-operational-schedules}
  
  \paragraph{7.1 Design Philosophy}\label{design-philosophy}
  
  Structured schedules are essential for crew health, morale, and
  productivity. Research from all major analogues confirms:
  
  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{ISS:} Highly structured 16-hour work / 8-hour sleep schedule
    prevents drift
  \item
    \textbf{MARS-500:} Unstructured time led to crew becoming
    progressively inactive; 4 of 6 developed sleep disorders
  \item
    \textbf{Biosphere 2:} Crew faction formation was exacerbated by
    disagreements over daily priorities
  \item
    \textbf{HI-SEAS:} Crew performed 40 tasks/week; structured activities
    maintained engagement
  \item
    \textbf{Concordia:} Circadian disruption during polar night requires
    strict light/dark scheduling
  \end{itemize}
  
  \paragraph{7.2 Daily Schedule (Sol = 24 h 37
  min)}\label{daily-schedule-sol-24-h-37-min}
  
  The daily schedule follows a \textbf{16/8 rhythm} (16 h active, 8 h
  sleep), with solar time adjusted for Mars’s slightly longer day:
  
