  \subsubsection{15. Conclusion}\label{conclusion-1}
  
  Version 2.0 of the Mars Rubber-Glass House transforms the original
  concept from a structural feasibility study into a comprehensive design
  for indefinite human habitation on Mars. The key advances are:
  
  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
  \item
    \textbf{From shelter to ecosystem} — The closed-loop life support
    system with waste-to-resource cycling creates a micro-biosphere where
    human waste feeds crops that feed humans, approaching the ideal of a
    self-sustaining system.
  \item
    \textbf{From blueprint to operations manual} — Structured daily,
    weekly, monthly, and annual routines, informed by decades of analogue
    experience, address the human factors that make or break isolated
    communities.
  \item
    \textbf{From Earth-dependent to Mars-native} — The ISRU strategy
    progressively reduces dependence on Earth resupply, consistent with
    the Three-Step Evolution roadmap toward autonomous structures
    (\href{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16540444}{DOI:
    10.5281/zenodo.16540444}).
  \item
    \textbf{From theoretical to validated} — The five-stage simulation
    methodology ensures every system is continuously investigated,
    modelled, simulated, validated, and scientifically assessed.
  \end{enumerate}
  
  The approximately \$3 M materials cost (or \textasciitilde\$108 M
  including transport) positions the Mars Rubber-Glass House as a serious
  candidate for early Martian settlement. As ISRU capabilities mature and
  transport costs decline — SpaceX targets \$200/kg to LEO within the
  decade — the economics become increasingly favourable.
  
  \begin{quote}
  \emph{“A house is not a home until it is lived in. On Mars, a house is
  not a home until it can sustain the lives within it — indefinitely,
  gracefully, and with dignity.”}
  \end{quote}
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
