#### 9. Medical and Psychological Support

##### 9.1 Medical Facility

Located in the temperate zone, the medical bay occupies ~15 m²:

| Equipment | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Portable ultrasound | Diagnostic imaging (replaces X-ray — no radiation source needed) |
| Blood analyser (point-of-care) | CBC, metabolic panel, HbA1c, CRP, electrolytes |
| Portable DXA scanner | Bone density monitoring (critical for Mars gravity: 0.38 g) |
| Surgical kit (minor) | Wound repair, tooth extraction, abscess drainage |
| Pharmacy cabinet | Antibiotics, analgesics, anaesthetics, cardiac drugs, antidepressants |
| Defibrillator + AED | Cardiac emergency |
| Telemedicine terminal | Video consultation with Earth (4–24 min delay) |
| Psychological assessment tools | PHQ-9 (depression), GAD-7 (anxiety), PSQI (sleep quality), crew sociometry |

##### 9.2 Psychological Support Framework

Drawing on lessons from all analogues:

| Stressor | Mitigation | Evidence |
|---|---|---|
| **Isolation** | Weekly Earth video calls; crew social events; varied daily activities | MARS-500: crew spent increasing time alone; ISS: scheduled family calls essential |
| **Confinement** | 5 m ceiling height; glass walls with exterior views; varied thermal zones; EVA opportunities | Submarines/Antarctic: perceived spaciousness correlates with morale |
| **Interpersonal conflict** | Conflict resolution training pre-mission; private cabins; quiet room; monthly psychology review | Biosphere 2: crew split into two factions; prevention is key |
| **Monotony** | Rotating task assignments; hobby supplies; musical instruments; virtual reality recreation; celebration of holidays and milestones | HI-SEAS: 40 tasks/week maintained engagement; celebrations important |
| **Circadian disruption** | Strict light scheduling (blue-light curfew at 20:00); dedicated sleep cabins with blackout; morning bright-light exposure | Concordia + MARS-500: circadian disruption is universal in isolation |
| **Loss of purpose** | Clear mission objectives; science publication goals; shared responsibility for food production; mentoring roles | ISS: crew who feel scientifically productive report higher satisfaction |
| **Health anxiety** | Regular medical monitoring; telemedicine access; comprehensive pharmacy; cross-training in basic medical skills for all crew | All analogues: medical training for non-doctors is essential |

##### 9.3 Crew Selection Criteria

Based on analogue research, the 4-person crew should include:

| Role | Skills | Backup Competence |
|---|---|---|
| **Commander / Engineer** | Leadership, mechanical/electrical engineering | Basic agriculture, first aid |
| **Flight Surgeon / Biologist** | Medicine, biology, psychology | Laboratory techniques, emergency repair |
| **Agricultural Scientist** | Botany, aquaculture, food science | Water systems, composting |
| **Geologist / ISRU Specialist** | Geology, chemistry, robotics | EVA specialist, communication systems |

All crew must be cross-trained in: emergency medicine, fire suppression, EVA operations, basic repair of all critical systems.

***

#### 10. Safety and Emergency Systems

##### 10.1 Emergency Scenarios and Responses

| Scenario | Detection | Response | Shelter Time |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Rapid depressurisation** | Pressure sensors (< 65 kPa alarm) | Seal affected zone; don emergency masks; locate and patch breach | Minutes |
| **Fire** | Smoke + thermal sensors | CO_{2} extinguishers; seal compartment; ventilate after extinguishing | Minutes–hours |
| **Solar particle event** | Space weather alert (8 min warning from Earth) | Crew to storm shelter; remain until all-clear | 24–72 hours |
| **Dust storm (major)** | Atmospheric opacity sensors | Load priority mode; LED agriculture; battery conservation | Weeks |
| **Medical emergency** | Crew report; vital monitors | Stabilise; telemedicine consultation; surgical intervention if trained | — |
| **Communication loss** | Signal timeout | Autonomous operations per standing protocols; antenna diagnostic | Days–weeks |
| **Water system failure** | Flow + quality sensors | Switch to emergency reservoir (10 m³ = 100 days at reduced use) | Days |
| **Crop failure** | Visual + sensor monitoring | Emergency rations (6 months stored); accelerated replanting | Weeks–months |

##### 10.2 Emergency Consumables

| Item | Quantity | Duration (4 crew) |
|---|---|---|
| Emergency food rations | 2,400 kg (freeze-dried) | 180 days |
| Water reserve (underfloor) | 10,000 L | 100 days (at 25 L/person/day) |
| Oxygen candles (backup) | 200 units | 60 days |
| Medical supplies | Full pharmacy | 2 years |
| Spare parts (critical systems) | Comprehensive kit | — |

***

