  \subsection{The 1000 m² Mars Rubber-Glass House — Version
  2.0}\label{the-1000-muxb2-mars-rubber-glass-house-version-2.0}
  
  \subsubsection{Abstract}\label{abstract-1}
  
  This paper presents the Version 2.0 design of the 1000 m² Mars
  Rubber-Glass House, a comprehensive upgrade from the structural
  feasibility study (v1, June 2024) into a fully specified habitat for
  long-duration human habitation. Building on the Design Improvements v2
  proposals, v2.0 integrates five major new systems: closed-loop
  waste-to-resource cycling, structured crew routines informed by Earth
  analogue experience, in-situ resource utilisation (ISRU), a medical and
  psychological support framework, and a simulation and validation
  methodology. The result transforms the Rubber-Glass House from a shelter
  into a self-sustaining micro-civilisation capable of supporting four
  crew members indefinitely.
  
  \begin{center}\rule{0.5\linewidth}{0.5pt}\end{center}
  
  \subsubsection{1. Introduction}\label{introduction-2}
  
  \paragraph{1.1 Background}\label{background}
  
  The original Mars Rubber-Glass House (v1) established the structural and
  material foundation: a 1000 m² (50 m × 20 m) single-storey habitat with
  silicone rubber foundations, a titanium/Inconel frame, borosilicate
  glass walls, rooftop solar panels, and a single airlock — all at a
  materials cost of approximately \$1.02 M
  (\href{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16493055}{DOI:
  10.5281/zenodo.16493055}).
  
  The Design Improvements v2 document identified critical gaps in the v1
  design and proposed eight upgrades:
  
  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
  \tightlist
  \item
    Interior height increase from 3 m to 5 m
  \item
    Three thermal zones (warm, temperate, cool)
  \item
    Closed-loop water management
  \item
    Multi-layered radiation shielding
  \item
    Agricultural system expansion with vertical farming
  \item
    Airlock redundancy (2 + emergency hatch)
  \item
    Energy storage and redundancy (100 kWh battery + RHU)
  \item
    Acoustics and crew privacy (individual cabins)
  \end{enumerate}
  
  \paragraph{1.2 Scope of Version 2.0}\label{scope-of-version-2.0}
  
  Version 2.0 accepts all eight v2 proposals as baseline and extends the
  design into a \textbf{complete operational habitat} by addressing five
  previously unspecified domains:
  
  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{Closed-loop waste-to-resource cycling} — from human excreta
    and food waste to fertiliser, water, and chemical feedstocks
  \item
    \textbf{Structured crew routines} — daily, weekly, monthly, and annual
    schedules informed by Biosphere 2, MARS-500, HI-SEAS, ISS, and
    Antarctic station operations
  \item
    \textbf{In-situ resource utilisation (ISRU)} — manufacturing
    construction materials, propellants, and speciality chemicals from
    Martian resources
  \item
    \textbf{Medical and psychological support} — informed by
    confined-environment psychology research
  \item
    \textbf{Simulation and validation methodology} — a rigorous cycle of
    Investigation → Model → Simulation → Validation → Scientific
    Assessment
  \end{itemize}
  
