  \subsubsection{3. Closed-Loop Life Support System
  (CLLSS)}\label{closed-loop-life-support-system-cllss}
  
  The v2.0 habitat operates as a near-closed bioregenerative life-support
  system. The goal is \textbf{≥ 95\% closure} for water and \textbf{≥ 80\%
  closure} for food, following the Biosphere 2 model but improved with 30
  years of subsequent research.
  
  \paragraph{3.1 Atmospheric Management}\label{atmospheric-management}
  
  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}
    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3333}}
    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3333}}
    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3333}}@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
  Parameter
  \end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
  Target
  \end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
  Method
  \end{minipage} \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  O\_\{2\} partial pressure & 21.0 kPa (± 0.5) & Photosynthesis +
  electrolysis backup \\
  CO\_\{2\} partial pressure & 0.04–0.10 kPa & Plant uptake + scrubber
  fallback \\
  Total pressure & 70 kPa & Pressure vessel + relief valves \\
  Relative humidity & 40–60\% & Condensation + dehumidification \\
  Temperature (warm zone) & 22–24 °C & Active heating + thermal mass \\
  Trace gas removal & \textless{} SMAC limits & Activated carbon +
  catalytic oxidiser \\
  \end{longtable}
  
  \textbf{Lesson from Biosphere 2:} O\_\{2\} declined from 20.9\% to
  14.5\% over 16 months due to microbial respiration in organic-rich soils
  and CO\_\{2\} absorption by unsealed concrete. V2.0 mitigates this
  through: - Sealed silicone rubber floor preventing soil–concrete
  reactions - Dedicated algae bioreactors (40 m²) optimised for O\_\{2\}
  production - Backup electrolysis unit (splitting water → H\_\{2\} +
  O\_\{2\}) - Real-time atmospheric monitoring with automated adjustment
  
  \paragraph{3.2 Water Management}\label{water-management}
  
  \textbf{Water budget per crew member (with recycling):}
  
  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}lll@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  Source & Volume/day & Notes \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  Drinking water & 2.5 L & Potable grade \\
  Food preparation & 1.0 L & \\
  Personal hygiene & 6.0 L & Shower (Navy-style, 2 min) \\
  Laundry & 5.0 L & Weekly, shared \\
  Agriculture irrigation & 8.0 L & Recirculating hydroponic \\
  Toilet flush / processing & 2.5 L & Vacuum-assist \\
  \textbf{Total per person} & \textbf{25 L/day} & \\
  \textbf{Total for 4 crew} & \textbf{100 L/day} & \\
  \end{longtable}
  
  \textbf{Water recycling system (modelled on ISS WRS):}
  
  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
  \tightlist
  \item
    \textbf{Humidity condensate recovery} — Air handlers collect
    \textasciitilde4 L/day per person from transpiration and respiration
  \item
    \textbf{Greywater filtration} — Shower, laundry, and kitchen water
    filtered through biofilm reactor + activated carbon + UV sterilisation
  \item
    \textbf{Urine processing} — Vapour compression distillation (VCD)
    achieving 93\% recovery rate
  \item
    \textbf{Plant transpiration recovery} — The agricultural zone alone
    recycles \textasciitilde60 L/day through plant transpiration →
    condensation
  \item
    \textbf{Underfloor reservoir} — 10 m³ emergency reserve integrated
    into silicone rubber floor slab (inherently watertight)
  \end{enumerate}
  
  \textbf{Target water recovery rate:} ≥ 93\% (ISS demonstrated),
  aspiration ≥ 97\% with plant transpiration loop.
  
  \paragraph{3.3 Waste-to-Resource
  Cycling}\label{waste-to-resource-cycling}
  
  This is a \textbf{key innovation of v2.0}. Every waste stream is treated
  as a resource input:
  
  \subparagraph{3.3.1 Human Excreta
  Processing}\label{human-excreta-processing}
  
  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}
    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}
    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}
    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}
    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
  Waste Stream
  \end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
  Daily Volume (4 crew)
  \end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
  Processing
  \end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
  Output
  \end{minipage} \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  \textbf{Urine} & \textasciitilde6 L & VCD → water; urea extraction →
  nitrogen fertiliser & \textasciitilde5.5 L water + \textasciitilde30 g
  urea/day \\
  \textbf{Faeces} & \textasciitilde0.6 kg & Thermophilic composting (60–70
  °C, 14 days) → vermicomposting (21 days) & \textasciitilde0.15 kg
  stabilised compost/day \\
  \textbf{Greywater sludge} & \textasciitilde2 L & Biofilm digestion →
  nutrient extraction & Phosphorus + potassium salts \\
  \end{longtable}
  
  \textbf{Composting process:} 1. \textbf{Collection} — Vacuum toilet
  separates urine and faeces at source 2. \textbf{Pre-treatment} — Faeces
  mixed with crop residue (C:N ratio 25:1–30:1) 3. \textbf{Thermophilic
  composting} — Enclosed reactor at 60–70 °C for 14 days; kills pathogens
  (Salmonella, E. coli, helminth ova) 4. \textbf{Vermicomposting} —
  \emph{Eisenia fetida} (red wiggler worms) process compost for additional
  21 days, producing nutrient-rich vermicast 5. \textbf{Maturation} — 30
  days aerobic curing 6. \textbf{Application} — Distributed to
  agricultural beds as slow-release fertiliser
  
  \textbf{Safety:} The thermophilic stage exceeds WHO guidelines for
  pathogen destruction (≥ 55 °C for ≥ 14 days). Compost is tested for
  faecal coliforms before agricultural use.
  
