  repairs, ISRU & Temperate \\
  15:30–15:45 & 15 min & Break & — \\
  15:45–17:00 & 1.25 h & \textbf{EVA or training} (2 crew, alternating
  pairs) or indoor exercise & Various \\
  17:00–18:00 & 1 h & \textbf{Mandatory exercise} — resistance training +
  cardio & Warm \\
  18:00–18:30 & 30 min & Personal hygiene + shower & Warm \\
  18:30–19:15 & 45 min & Dinner (communal) & Warm \\
  19:15–20:00 & 45 min & \textbf{Evening briefing} — log entries, next-sol
  planning, Earth video uplink & Temperate \\
  20:00–22:00 & 2 h & \textbf{Personal time} — recreation, reading, video
  calls, hobbies & Warm / Cabins \\
  22:00–06:00 & 8 h & \textbf{Sleep} (individual cabins, blue-light-free)
  & Cabins \\
  \end{longtable}
  
  \textbf{Critical design features:} - \textbf{Three communal meals} —
  Biosphere 2 and HI-SEAS both found shared meals are the most important
  social bonding activity - \textbf{Mandatory exercise} — ISS protocol: 2
  h/day (1 h resistance + 1 h cardio) prevents bone loss
  (\textasciitilde1.5\%/month) and muscle atrophy - \textbf{Morning
  medical check} — Body weight, blood pressure, pulse oximetry, mood
  self-assessment on a 1–10 scale (MARS-500 found early detection of
  psychological issues is critical) - \textbf{Personal time} — Minimum 2
  h/day unscheduled time (ISS finding: overscheduling causes crew
  rebellion — see Skylab 4 “strike”) - \textbf{Blue-light curfew} — No
  blue-spectrum lighting after 20:00 (Concordia research shows circadian
  disruption is a major issue)
  
  \paragraph{7.3 Weekly Schedule}\label{weekly-schedule}
  
  Each sol has a primary focus. The week is structured around a
  \textbf{6-day work / 1-day rest} cycle (consistent with ISS operations):
  
