  \textbf{Proposal: Design of a 1000 m² Rubber-Glass House for Mars}
  
  Dear Sir or Madam,\\
  Mars represents the next great frontier of human exploration. To
  successfully advance the settlement of Mars, the development of
  innovative, sustainable, and safe habitats is crucial. We present to you
  a unique concept: a 1000 m² rubber-glass house, specifically designed
  for the extreme conditions of Mars. This project offers an exceptional
  investment opportunity in the future of space travel and
  extraterrestrial colonization.
  
  \subsection{1. Concept and Design}\label{concept-and-design}
  
  \textbf{Floor Plan and Structure} - \textbf{Total Area:} 1000 m² (50 m x
  20 m) - \textbf{Height:} 3 m, sufficient for living and working spaces
  
  \textbf{Materials} - \textbf{Rubber floor slab and foundation wall:}
  Cast from silicone rubber on-site - \textbf{Frame:} High-strength
  titanium or Inconel structure - \textbf{Glazing:} Borosilicate glass
  with an insulation layer - \textbf{Roof:} Solar panels for energy
  generation - \textbf{Airlock:} Titanium/Inconel construction with
  borosilicate glass
  
  \subsection{2. Detailed Description}\label{detailed-description}
  
  \textbf{Rubber Floor Slab and Foundation Wall} - \textbf{Material:}
  Silicone rubber to ensure flexibility and airtightness -
  \textbf{Dimensions:} - Floor slab: 1000 m², thickness 10 cm - Foundation
  wall: 140 m circumference, 50 cm height, 10 cm thickness -
  \textbf{Volume:} 107 m³ of silicone rubber
  
  \textbf{Titanium or Inconel Frame} - \textbf{Weight:} 5500 kg including
  fastenings (rivets or screws) - \textbf{Advantages:} Excellent corrosion
  resistance, high strength, and thermal stability
  
  \textbf{Borosilicate Glass with Insulation Layer} - \textbf{Area:} 1000
  m² - \textbf{Weight:} 10,000 kg - \textbf{Coatings:} Anti-reflective and
  UV protection coatings to improve energy efficiency and durability
  
  \textbf{Solar Panels on the Roof} - \textbf{Area:} 1000 m² -
  \textbf{Energy Generation:} Stable at approximately 973,392 kWh per
  Martian year, enough to supply the living areas and systems
  
  \textbf{Airlock} - \textbf{Dimensions:} 2 m x 2 m x 5 m -
  \textbf{Weight:} approx. 1000 kg - \textbf{Material:} Titanium/Inconel
  and borosilicate glass
  
  \subsection{3. Internal Equipment}\label{internal-equipment}
  
  \textbf{Agricultural Areas and CO2 Conversion} - \textbf{Agricultural
  Area:} 50 m² per person, totaling 200 m² for a vegan diet -
  \textbf{Plants:} Vegetables, fruits, legumes, grains - \textbf{CO2 to O2
  Conversion:} Plants and algae reactors, area approx. 20 m²
  
  \textbf{Living Quarters and Laboratories} - \textbf{Layout:} Living
  areas, laboratories, and machinery room for up to 4 people -
  \textbf{Facilities:} Modern equipment to ensure livelihood and research
  
  \subsection{4. Sustainability and
  Efficiency}\label{sustainability-and-efficiency}
  
  \textbf{Utilization of Locally Produced Materials} - \textbf{Silicone
  rubber and silicate glass:} Manufactured from Martian rock and silicon
